# Final Project for CISC 106

# UD Bus Tracker

Live University of Delaware bus wait times, and a planner that tells you
whether to walk or take the bus.

## Table of Contents
- [Description](#description)
- [Installation](#installation)
- [Usage](#usage)
- [Pages](#pages)
- [Data](#data)
- [Map paths](#map-paths)
- [Deploying](#deploying)

## Description
The goal of this project is to determine whether it is better to walk to a
class/destination or to take the bus at the University of Delaware campus.
The site shows the 5 stops with the shortest and longest waits, which routes
are not running, and plans a trip between two campus places.

## Installation
```bash
pip install -r src/requirements.txt
```
The only package needed is drafter 2.1.3; everything else is the Python
standard library. Drafter runs the site inside the browser (Pyodide), so
`requests` and other socket-based packages would not work there.

## Usage
```bash
cd scripts
python main.py
```
Drafter opens the site at http://localhost:8000. The 87 unit tests run every
time the file loads. The debug panel is hidden (`hide_debug_information()`),
so their results print in the terminal; remove that line in `main.py` to
see them under the page again.

Drafter writes errors to `scripts/drafter-debug.log` (git-ignored). With
drafter 2.1.3 it always logs "Unhandled configuration update event" for the
`set_site_information` fields; that is a drafter issue and harmless.

## Pages
- **Home**: data status and how many routes are running.
- **Fastest stops / Slowest stops**: top 5 stops by wait for the next bus.
- **Route status**: running and not-running routes.
- **Plan a trip**: short how-to, and a map with a pin for every choice in
  the two lists (campus places and every bus stop, with matching names).
- **Trip result**: whether to walk or take the bus, numbered directions
  (walk to the stop, wait, ride, get off, walk), and a map with every bus
  stop (yellow dots), the walk straight there (black dashed), the walks to
  and from the bus stops (purple dashed), the bus ride (solid, in the
  route's color) and, faint, the way the bus drives into your stop.
- **Refresh**: reloads live data.

## Data
Bus data comes from the public ETA SPOT JSON service used by UD's bus app
(`https://udshuttle.etaspot.net/service.php`). The site loads it at start and
reloads it whenever a page is opened with data older than 60 seconds, since
arrival times count down from the moment they were loaded. Walking times use
5 km/h along real walking paths. Campus place coordinates are approximate.

## Map paths
Map paths are precomputed once, so the site never calls a routing
service. `scripts/build_paths.py` makes two files with the free
OpenStreetMap router (routing.openstreetmap.de), one request per second:

- `scripts/walking_paths.json`: the walking path and its length for every
  pair of trip planner points (campus places and bus stops).
- `scripts/bus_paths.json`: for each bus route, its color, its stops in
  the order the bus visits them (from ETA SPOT's `get_patterns`), and the
  driving path from each stop to the next. The bus ride on the map follows
  these in order, so it always runs in the bus's real direction.

```bash
cd scripts
python build_paths.py
```

Re-run it after adding a place to `PLACES` or when UD changes its stops or
routes; it only fetches missing walking paths (the bus paths take about a
minute). Anything missing is drawn as a straight line.

The trip map uses Leaflet 1.9.4 (from unpkg) and OpenStreetMap tiles,
so it needs an internet connection.

## Deploying
`.github/workflows/deploy.yml` publishes the site to GitHub Pages. It
installs drafter, builds the site with `drafter main.py --compile` (into
`scripts/dist`, git-ignored) and uploads it. `main.py` registers
`walking_paths.json` and `bus_paths.json` with `add_website_file`, so the
published site gets them too.

One-time setup: in the repository's Settings > Pages, set Source to
GitHub Actions. After that, every push to `master` deploys, or run it by
hand from the Actions tab (Deploy main branch as website > Run workflow).
